/* Logic:

1. Initialize `l = 0` and `r = 0` to represent the range of indices reachable with the current number of jumps.

2. Initialize `jumps = 0` to count the minimum jumps required.

3. Run a while loop until `r` reaches or crosses the last index (`n - 1`).

4. Initialize `farthest = 0` to track the maximum index we can reach with one additional jump.

5. Traverse all indices from `l` to `r`:

- Calculate the maximum reachable index from each position using `i + nums[i]`.
- Update `farthest` with the maximum value.

6. After exploring the current range:

- Update `l = r + 1` to start the next range.
- Update `r = farthest` to extend the reachable boundary.
- Increment `jumps` because we have used one more jump.

7. Repeat until the last index is reachable.

8. Return `jumps`.

### Core greedy intuition

Instead of jumping immediately to the next index, explore all positions reachable within the current jump and choose the maximum possible reach for the next jump.

This ensures we reach the last index using the minimum number of jumps.

Complexity:

- Time: O(n)
- Space: O(1)
*/

class Solution {
public:
    int jump(vector<int>& nums) {
        int n = nums.size();

        // l and r represent the current range of indices
        // that we can reach using the current number of jumps.
        int l = 0, r = 0;

        // Counts the minimum number of jumps required.
        int jumps = 0;

        // Continue until we can reach the last index.
        while (r < n - 1) {

            // Stores the maximum index reachable
            // with one additional jump.
            int farthest = 0;

            // Explore all indices in the current reachable range.
            for (int i = l; i <= r; i++) {

                // Calculate the farthest index we can reach
                // by jumping from index i.
                farthest = max(farthest, i + nums[i]);
            }

            // The next range starts just after the current range.
            l = r + 1;

            // Update the right boundary to the farthest reachable index.
            r = farthest;

            // One jump is completed.
            jumps++;
        }

        // Return the minimum number of jumps.
        return jumps;
    }
};
